  "slug": "quantum-learning-5545",
  "summary": "Preprints probe how encoding, dephasing and readout set memory and nonlinearity in quantum reservoirs, alongside recurrent and learned models for Hamiltonian inference.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how input encoding, noise and readout determine computational capacity in fixed-dynamics quantum models. Data re-uploading is reported to improve higher-order nonlinear processing and chaotic-series tasks in physical reservoirs. Two spin-chain studies treat dephasing as a resource: one simulates a six-qubit reservoir where dephasing and observable choice affect NARMA, limit-cycle and chaotic forecasting, the other proposes a minimum-complexity open XX chain with pure dephasing. Multi-layer quantum extreme learning machines with MMD-fitted linear readouts are proposed for generative modeling, while variational quantum recurrent networks are benchmarked for memory and nonlinear capacity against classical baselines. Adjacent work applies recurrent quantum RL and JEPA-based world models to Hamiltonian identification, and randomized real-time dynamics to neural-network quantum states.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
